content:
create a scraping bot that can input websites and scrape anything from images, fonts, pdfs, products, etc. This is a proof of concept project.  

1. Mindset for a POC 
Since this is just a demo, you don’t need a full crawler or infra. Aim for:
* Input: a single URL (e.g. the CBOS product page).
* Backend: call Parse.bot (or similar) once, ask it for HTML and a basic structure.
* Post‑process: extract images, internal links, headings, pricing blocks.
* Output: dump to JSON or render in a super simple UI (static HTML, a Jupyter notebook, or a single‑file web app).
The “magic” for the demo is showing that from one URL, you can automatically:
* List all product/framework cards.
* Show all images used on the page.
* Show all internal links (e.g. “Creative Business Workspace”, “All Products”, “FAQs”).
2. Use a scraping API instead of rolling your own 
Services like Parse.bot, ScraperAPI, Zyte, etc. all work similarly:
1. You send your target URL to their API.
2. They handle headless browser, JS rendering, proxies, bot detection, etc.
3. They return HTML, or sometimes a pre‑parsed document/JSON.
For a proof of concept, you can:
* Keep all the heavy lifting on their side.
* Use minimal code on your side to parse what they return.
I’ll show an example in Node and Python using a generic “scrape this URL and give me HTML” style API. You can adapt to Parse.bot’s exact syntax once you have their docs.
3. Minimal flow with a scraping API + parsing 
Assume the scraping API looks like this (pseudo):
* Endpoint: ‎`POST https://api.parse.bot/scrape`
* Body: ‎`{ "url": "https://example.com" }`
* Response: ‎`{ "html": "<!doctype html>..." }`
Node example (using axios + cheerio) 
This script:
* Sends the URL to Parse.bot.
* Receives HTML.
* Extracts:
   * All images (‎`img[src]`).
   * All links (‎`a[href]`) and filters to same domain.
   * Headings and price‑looking text.

Run:
You now have a single JSON file with all images, internal links, headings, and price‑ish sections from that CBOS page.
Python version (requests + BeautifulSoup) 
Same idea in Python, if that’s more comfortable:
This gives you a clear “bot” story:
* “It calls an API to render the page.”
* “Then it extracts all images, internal links, and product blocks.”
* “Here’s the JSON we get back from a CBOS‑like landing page.”
4. Extending to “linked resources” for the demo 
If you want one step more “bot‑like” without going full crawler:
1. Run the above on the main CBOS page.
2. Take a subset of internal links that look like product/learn pages (‎`/products/`, ‎`/pages/`).
3. Loop through those links and repeat the same fetch‑and‑extract.
4. Accumulate results as an array of page objects.
Conceptually:
That’s enough to call it a “scraper bot” in a POC deck without investing in real crawling infra.
5. How this plays in a demo narrative 
For a quick presentation or Loom:
* Show original CBOS page.
* Show the one‑file script.
* Run it, open ‎`demo_output.json`, and visually point to:
   * The list of images the bot discovered.
   * The list of internal links (e.g. “Boring Issues”, “Creative Business Workspace”).
   * The pricing/framework blocks it found.
That’s all you need for a credible, focused “backend scraper” proof of concept that uses a service like Parse.bot instead of you dealing with headless browsers and captchas yourself.

// demo-scraper.js
// filename: demo-scraper.js
import axios from "axios";
import * as cheerio from "cheerio";
import { URL } from "url";
import fs from "fs";
const PARSEBOT_API_KEY = process.env.PARSEBOT_API_KEY;
const TARGET_URL = "https://boringstudios.com.au/products/creative-business-operating-system"; // example
async function fetchWithParseBot(url) {
  try {
    const res = await axios.post(
      "https://api.parse.bot/scrape", // replace with real endpoint
      { url },
      {
        headers: {
          Authorization: `Bearer ${PARSEBOT_API_KEY}`,
          "Content-Type": "application/json",
        },
        timeout: 30000,
      }
    );
    return res.data.html; // adjust to the real response shape
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Parse.bot error:", err.response?.data || err.message);
    throw err;
  }
}
function extractData(html, baseUrl) {
  const $ = cheerio.load(html);
  const base = new URL(baseUrl);
  const domain = base.hostname;
  // Images
  const images = [];
  $("img[src]").each((_, el) => {
    const src = $(el).attr("src");
    if (!src) return;
    const abs = new URL(src, baseUrl).toString();
    images.push({
      src: abs,
      alt: $(el).attr("alt") || "",
    });
  });
  // Links (internal)
  const links = [];
  $("a[href]").each((_, el) => {
    const href = $(el).attr("href");
    if (!href) return;
    const abs = new URL(href, baseUrl).toString();
    const parsed = new URL(abs);
    if (parsed.hostname === domain) {
      links.push({
        href: abs.split("#")[0],
        text: $(el).text().trim().replace(/\s+/g, " "),
      });
    }
  });
  // Headings
  const headings = [];
  ["h1", "h2", "h3"].forEach((tag) => {
    $(tag).each((_, el) => {
      const text = $(el).text().trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
      if (text) {
        headings.push({
          level: tag,
          text,
        });
      }
    });
  });
  // Super basic “product / pricing block” extraction: scan sections for $ amounts
  const blocks = [];
  $("section").each((_, el) => {
    const text = $(el).text().trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
    if (!text) return;
    const priceMatch = text.match(/\$\d[\d,]*(\.\d{2})?/);
    if (priceMatch) {
      blocks.push({
        snippet: text.slice(0, 300),
        price: priceMatch[0],
      });
    }
  });
  return {
    url: baseUrl,
    title: $("title").text().trim(),
    headings,
    images,
    links,
    blocks,
  };
}
async function run() {
  const html = await fetchWithParseBot(TARGET_URL);
  const data = extractData(html, TARGET_URL);
  fs.writeFileSync("demo-output.json", JSON.stringify(data, null, 2), "utf-8");
  console.log("Saved demo-output.json");
}
run().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e);
  process.exit(1);
});

# demo_scraper.py
import os
import json
from urllib.parse import urljoin, urlparse
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
PARSEBOT_API_KEY = os.getenv("PARSEBOT_API_KEY")
TARGET_URL = "https://boringstudios.com.au/products/creative-business-operating-system"  # example
def fetch_with_parsebot(url: str) -> str:
    resp = requests.post(
        "https://api.parse.bot/scrape",  # replace with real endpoint
        json={"url": url},
        headers={
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {PARSEBOT_API_KEY}",
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
        },
        timeout=30,
    )
    resp.raise_for_status()
    data = resp.json()
    return data["html"]  # adjust field name to real response
def extract_data(html: str, base_url: str) -> dict:
    soup = BeautifulSoup(html, "html.parser")
    domain = urlparse(base_url).netloc
    # Images
    images = []
    for img in soup.find_all("img", src=True):
        src = img["src"]
        abs_src = urljoin(base_url, src)
        images.append(
            {
                "src": abs_src,
                "alt": img.get("alt", ""),
            }
        )
    # Links (internal)
    links = []
    for a in soup.find_all("a", href=True):
        href = a["href"]
        abs_href = urljoin(base_url, href)
        parsed = urlparse(abs_href)
        if parsed.scheme in {"http", "https"} and parsed.netloc == domain:
            links.append(
                {
                    "href": abs_href.split("#")[0],
                    "text": " ".join(a.get_text(strip=True).split()),
                }
            )
    # Headings
    headings = []
    for level in ["h1", "h2", "h3"]:
        for h in soup.find_all(level):
            text = " ".join(h.get_text(strip=True).split())
            if text:
                headings.append({"level": level, "text": text})
    # Pricing/product blocks
    blocks = []
    for section in soup.find_all("section"):
        text = " ".join(section.get_text(strip=True).split())
        if not text:
            continue
        # naive price detection
        import re
        match = re.search(r"\$\d[\d,]*(\.\d{2})?", text)
        if match:
            blocks.append(
                {
                    "snippet": text[:300],
                    "price": match.group(0),
                }
            )
    return {
        "url": base_url,
        "title": soup.title.string.strip() if soup.title else "",
        "headings": headings,
        "images": images,
        "links": links,
        "blocks": blocks,
    }
def main():
    html = fetch_with_parsebot(TARGET_URL)
    data = extract_data(html, TARGET_URL)
    with open("demo_output.json", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(data, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)
    print("Saved demo_output.json")
if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()

queue = [MAIN_PAGE]
visited = []
while queue not empty and visited < 10:
  url = queue.pop()
  html = fetchWithParseBot(url)
  data = extractData(html)
  save(data)
  visited.add(url)
  for link in data.links:
    if link.href looks like product/learn page and not in visited:
      queue.push(link.href)


